{
  // The site Worker and booking module are TypeScript; Serendipity joins
  // through the Worker's imports. All run on workerd and share its globals.
  //
  // The Worker conversion initially left this include on `**/*.js`, so tsc saw
  // cal plus one transitively imported Worker module and skipped the other 68.
  // contract-tests now executes `tsc --listFilesOnly` and compares the program
  // with the directory, making the scope part of the contract rather than a
  // comment. The completed migration permits no @ts-nocheck opt-outs.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "target": "esnext",
    "module": "esnext",
    // A .ts specifier is the only one that resolves in BOTH trees: node strips
    // types at runtime and wrangler bundles the same file, so an import has to
    // name the file that exists rather than the one a compiler would emit.
    // Nothing is emitted anyway (noEmit above).
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    // Node strips these files directly in contract tests and Wrangler bundles
    // the same source for workerd. Keep syntax inside the erasable intersection
    // so neither path depends on a TypeScript transform the other does not run.
    "erasableSyntaxOnly": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    // A handler that falls off the end returns `undefined` where the runtime
    // wants a Response, and the route table types its entries as `Function`, so
    // nothing downstream catches it. +0 diagnostics across all ten programs,
    // measured 2026-08-27, which is what made it worth setting everywhere
    // rather than here alone.
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    // Workers globals only. With the DOM lib loaded, its CacheStorage shadows
    // Cloudflare's and every `caches.default` reads as an error.
    "lib": ["esnext"],
    // Node module declarations type the native node:zlib Brotli API enabled by
    // nodejs_compat. Explicitly naming node keeps bun globals out; the Workers
    // runtime declarations still arrive through include below.
    "types": ["node"],
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    // All programs enable null checks separately from full strictness. The
    // initial noImplicitAny probe found 7,197 missing annotations repo-wide;
    // enabling it alongside null checks would have buried the actionable work.
    // Current debt lives in ts-worker-baseline.json, checked by
    // bun run typecheck:worker. The per-file baseline may only shrink.
    "strictNullChecks": true,
    "strict": false
  },
  // Paths are relative to THIS file, which lives in config/ — hence the `../`.
  // The generated runtime surface is a literal path rather than a glob, so a
  // missing file (typecheck run without its generator) fails on the include
  // instead of checking the Worker against no globals at all.
  //
  // SERENDIPITY IS NAMED HERE THOUGH TSC ALREADY REACHED IT, and the second
  // reader is why. It arrives in this program transitively, through the
  // Worker's imports, so `tsc -p` has always checked it and check-ts-coverage
  // has always counted it. tsgolint asks a DIFFERENT question: it walks up from
  // each file looking for a config that NAMES it, and until 2026-09-22 nothing
  // did, so it built a default program with the DOM lib for serendipity alone.
  // Measured on an unchanged tree: `oxlint --type-aware --type-check` reported
  // 308 errors there against tsc's 0, five of them the `caches.default`
  // shadowing the `lib` note above warns about, and the plain `--type-aware`
  // lint reported 0 findings where it now reports 5. So the type-aware lint had
  // been blind on that file since it landed, exactly as #709 was blind on the
  // whole tree before the root tsconfig.json existed.
  //
  // Reachable and named are separate properties, and only the first one has a
  // check. Adding a tree here moved neither ratchet and left coverage at 374
  // files across 10 programs; the include is what the LINT reads.
  // The site Worker's own config, named here for the reason
  // tsconfig.cf-garage.json gives: checked beside the Worker it configures, so a
  // misspelled binding option is a compile error (since 2026-09-28).
  //
  // counter/ is the aadhar-counter Worker, which hosts the Counter Durable
  // Object the site binds by script_name. It is a separate deploy on the SAME
  // runtime with the same compatibility settings, and its entry re-exports
  // src/worker/counter.ts, so it belongs to this program rather than one of
  // its own: a separate `tsc -p` would re-report that file's baselined
  // diagnostics without the ratchet that tolerates them.
  "include": [
    "../cloudflare.config.ts",
    "../wrangler.config.ts",
    "../cal/src/**/*.ts",
    "../counter/src/**/*.ts",
    "../src/worker/**/*.ts",
    "../serendipity/**/*.ts",
    "./.generated/workers-runtime.d.ts"
  ]
}
